  "generated_text": "1. First, we need to choose 3 boys out of 6. The number of ways to do this is given by the combination formula \\(\\binom{n}{k}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\binom{6}{3} = \\frac{6!}{3!(6-3)!} = \\frac{6 \\times 5 \\times 4}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 20\n   \\]\n\n2. Next, we need to choose 3 girls out of 8. The number of ways to do this is:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{8}{3} = \\frac{8!}{3!(8-3)!} = \\frac{8 \\times 7 \\times 6}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 56\n   \\]\n\n3. To find the total number of ways to form the team, we multiply the number of ways to choose the boys by the number of ways to choose the girls:\n   \\[\n   20 \\times 56 = 1120\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{1120}\\).",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[1120, '1120']",
    "gold_answer": "We are picking 3 boys out of 6, so there are $\\binom{6}{3} = 20$ options for the boys on the team.  We are picking 3 girls out of 8, so there are $\\binom{8}{3} = 56$ options for the girls on the team.  This gives a total of $20 \\times 56 = \\boxed{1120}$ choices.",
    "is_correct": true
  },
